# The floor `make cover` enforces, per package. The number is where the package is today,
# rounded down: a floor is a ratchet, so it may only move up. Raise one when its package goes
# up, never lower one to make a run green.
#
# The target is 95% for everything. Where a package cannot reach it, the line says why in one
# clause — and the reason is a fact about the code, not about the test suite.
#
# `cmd/acornfall` and `internal/update` move by a few tenths between runs, because their coverage
# comes from child processes writing counters as they exit: a test that re-execs the gateway (the
# restart switch) leaves a process alive until cleanup, and its counters may miss the merge. The
# floors sit below the low observation on purpose — a ratchet that flaps is worse than a loose one.

cmd/acornfall                      74
internal/control     80
internal/httpapi                    75
internal/netns                      51
internal/protocol   100
internal/ready       83
internal/sandbox     78
internal/supervise   84
internal/update                    79
